WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.213
- Hydrogen has a very special
place in the universe.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.683
It's the first element
on the periodic table.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:19.023
It is the most abundant
element in the universe.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.807
And when clean energy
advocates think about

00:00:22.807 --> 00:00:25.147
"What can we find which would be

00:00:25.147 --> 00:00:28.860
"as easy to use as fossil fuels?"

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.020
they think about hydrogen,

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.893
the fuel that will, when
burned, only produce water.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.770
- We like to imagine that if we just built

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.290
enough of these, and these,

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.113
we could ditch fossil
fuels and save the planet.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.440
Unfortunately, it's not that simple.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.360
There are some things where you just need

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.390
good old fashioned combustible fuel.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.613
Now, startups around the world are betting

00:00:56.613 --> 00:01:00.870
that hydrogen can become the
fuel of the 21st century.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.330
- Just like today, we use fossil fuels

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.970
for different applications
like power plants,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.930
cars, and trucks, and
trains, hydrogen will be used

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.950
for all this variety of
different applications.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.350
- Eventually the world's going
to have to care about CO2.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.880
As soon as you start to care about CO2,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.100
hydrogen becomes pretty interesting.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.860
- But turning the most common
element in the universe

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.930
into the new fuel of choice

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.380
is harder than it might seem.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.980
The first thing you need
to know about hydrogen

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.983
is that not all hydrogen is created equal.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.723
Allow us to introduce
the hydrogen rainbow.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.650
- Hydrogen is unique

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.890
because you can generate
it in many different ways.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.070
and the different ways
in which it is created

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.353
actually are defined by colors.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.670
- The rainbow scales from the
most environmentally friendly

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.543
to the least.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.170
- Gray hydrogen means taking natural gas

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.950
and converting that into hydrogen

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.870
while creating CO2
emissions in the process.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.120
Blue hydrogen is gray hydrogen

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.950
but with carbon capture instead.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.483
So there are no CO2 emissions

00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:17.490
that are put into the atmosphere.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:21.000
Instead, they're captured
and buried deep underground.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.870
There's also pink hydrogen

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:26.700
where you use nuclear
power to create hydrogen.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.510
- One color is prized
above them all, green.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.060
- Green hydrogen is the
hydrogen that everybody wants

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.090
because it's the hydrogen that
uses renewable electricity

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.720
to split water into hydrogen and oxygen.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.760
- If we could make enough green hydrogen,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.510
we'd have a clean fuel source
with incredible versatility,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.180
making electricity whenever we need it,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.710
powering heavy industry,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.710
and theoretically, even
replacing fossil fuels

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.503
in some of the most polluting
forms of transportation.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.040
Today though, most hydrogen is produced

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.800
on the polluting end of the spectrum.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.420
So, engineers are working on

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.553
getting green hydrogen up to speed.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.550
- At H2Pro, we're
developing a new technology

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.273
that splits water into
hydrogen and oxygen.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.260
The environment was always dear to me.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.580
I remember, as a kid, having
this poster in my room,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.017
the Earth, and it was written on it

00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:27.897
"Fragile, handle with care".

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.860
And being here is a tremendous opportunity

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.023
to actually do something about it.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.190
- Talmon Marco founded
and sold two app companies

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.900
for more than a billion dollars

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.150
before following his green dreams

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.883
and starting H2Pro in 2019.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.400
- The process of splitting water

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.410
has been known for over 200 years.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.310
You put two electrodes in a
glass of water with some salt

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.600
turn on the power,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.393
and you get hydrogen and oxygen bubbles.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:02.340
- Today, I was in my son classroom

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.080
and I showed them an experiment

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.780
of how we can split water very easily.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.600
I just took a nine-volt battery,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.150
placed in the water with some salt,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.240
and voila, you have hydrogen
and oxygen coming out.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.318
That's nice, but it's
very, very inefficient

00:04:18.318 --> 00:04:21.120
and therefore very, very costly.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.990
The hard thing is how to
make hydrogen efficiently

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.193
and with low cost.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.370
- Our technology is different

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.560
in the fact that it's more efficient

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.660
and it's cheaper to manufacture,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.890
resulting in cheaper
hydrogen for our customers.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.550
- That's made possible by
a fancy engineering trick.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.040
Conventional electrolyzers
use electricity,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.230
not just to create hydrogen molecules,

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.070
but also to separate out
unwanted oxygen molecules.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:55.070
H2Pro found a way to separate
the oxygen using heat instead.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.480
- We have the oxygen being released

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.060
just by raising the temperature.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.040
So we are releasing the oxygen
without applying any power.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.183
This is the reason why we can
be so much more efficient.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.863
This is the unique technology of H2Pro,

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.740
these specific electrodes themselves,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.600
they release the oxygen.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.480
The next level, when we're
going to make our factory,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.363
it's going to work in a very
similar approach like here.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.140
- H2Pro's fighting an uphill battle

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.990
just by virtue of being green.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:35.160
Electrolysis makes up just 2%
of hydrogen production today

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:39.210
and many investors didn't
see the potential for growth.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:42.780
- Initially, it was very, very difficult

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.910
to raise money for H2Pro.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.694
In fact, we had to reach
to over a hundred funds

00:05:47.694 --> 00:05:51.213
until we managed to
get the company funded.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:54.930
Scaling is never easy.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.623
Our system produces
500,000 times more hydrogen

00:05:58.623 --> 00:06:00.930
than where we were three years ago,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.033
but we still have a ways to go.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.580
- The ultimate test will be

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.700
whether H2Pro can make
its product cheaply enough

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.493
to race ahead of its more
established competition.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.630
- The long term goal for
hydrogen to become a viable fuel

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:19.350
is for it to cost between
a dollar or $2 a kilogram.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.460
We are nowhere close to that.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.640
We are probably in the
three, or four, or $5 range

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.680
for green hydrogen today.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.810
- We anticipate that our customers

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.340
will have a cost of about $1
per kilogram of green hydrogen

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.640
by the end of this decade.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.620
- Companies like H2Pro
still have a long way to go

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.490
before they're producing
green hydrogen at scale,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.243
but being green isn't
the only way to be green.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:47.720
That'll make sense in a minute.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.680
- We describe what we are doing at C-Zero

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.450
as turquoise hydrogen,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.220
'cause it's kind of a combination

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.660
of both blue and green

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.710
in that it's low cost and low emission.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.020
C-Zero has developed a technology

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.773
for removing the carbon in natural gas.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.440
Natural gas is a hydrocarbon;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.450
it's made out of hydrogen and carbon,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:19.653
and what we do is remove the carbon,

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.220
so we pull out the solid carbon

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.200
out of the hydrogen that we produce

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.960
and there are no direct CO2 emissions

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.060
because anything that would've been CO2

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.533
is instead solid carbon.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.490
If you want to get hydrogen from water,

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:37.950
it takes seven and a
half times as much energy

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.610
to go pull hydrogen off of
oxygen by splitting water

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.370
as it does to pull hydrogen off of carbon

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:45.633
through our process.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.880
- The way C-Zero achieves
it is using molten salts.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.370
It has a big chamber full of salt

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.980
that is heated to a very high temperature.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.180
- So this is an example

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.090
of one of our laboratory scale reactors.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.700
So right here, we have
a very high temperature,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.820
around 1,000 degrees and slightly above,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:06.993
molten media column.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.820
- And it bubbles natural
gas through that chamber,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.670
leaving behind carbon
in its elemental form

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.523
and hydrogen as a gas that
can be trapped and sold.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.730
- If we're successful, we're producing

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.440
piles of black carbon

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:23.970
that ultimately it gets sequestered

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.410
back into the ground.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.320
You can think of it almost as
pre-combustion carbon capture.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.810
Rather than ending up with CO2

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.330
that you have to concentrate, compress,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.120
put into a pipeline and
inject it underground,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.350
we pull it out ahead of time

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.430
as a solid that's much denser
and you can move around

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:44.463
with conventional solids handling
equipment and dispose of.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.920
- Just like green hydrogen,

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.750
the success of turquoise hydrogen

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.520
will ultimately come down to cost.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.910
- $1.50 per kilogram of hydrogen

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.703
is our target for doing
this at very large scale.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.180
If you can be $2 a kilogram or less

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.360
with very low to zero CO2 emissions,

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.283
that's kind of a game changer.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.250
- The tough economics of hydrogen

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.440
can make it sound like we're a long way

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:13.083
from reaching the cleaner
end of the rainbow.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.837
And it's true that companies
like C-Zero and H2Pro

00:09:17.837 --> 00:09:22.020
are going to have to make
hydrogen at vastly larger scales

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.113
before they can make a dent
in fossil fuel consumption.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.670
But some industrial
companies aren't waiting

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:33.030
for the economics to be perfect
before making the switch,

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:36.003
even in one of the most polluting
industries in the world.

00:09:37.563 --> 00:09:42.090
- SSAB is a steel company
having a production capacity

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:47.090
of approximately 9 million tons
of steel products per year.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:52.400
We alone accounts for 10% of
Sweden's total CO2 emission.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:58.080
If we want to cut
emission, we can't continue

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.450
with the current
blast-furnace-based technology.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.820
We need to change technology.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.970
- So that's exactly what SSAB did,

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.120
developing a way of
replacing coal with hydrogen

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.430
in their steel making process.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.020
- In the process of making steel,

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.340
a factory has to convert
iron ore into iron.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.910
Iron ore is iron plus oxygen.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.100
The removal of oxygen requires,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.900
typically, the use of coal

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.200
which will combine with oxygen to form CO2

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.793
and warm the planet.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.160
You could also use green
hydrogen to take the oxygen away

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.983
and that will only produce water.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.690
- Our aim is to decarbonize
the whole value chain

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.930
from mining, to iron making, steel making,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.230
but also deliver our final
product to our customers

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.230
without using fossil fuel.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:53.640
That's the aim.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.980
We believe this is going to contribute

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.810
not only cutting our own emissions,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.200
but this technology can
also be used by others

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.513
when they are ready to take this step.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.170
- It's not going to be easy

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:10.200
for industries to take
full advantage of hydrogen,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.693
even when production costs come down.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.380
Storing and transporting the stuff

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.630
is something of a nightmare.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:21.480
And since hydrogen is extremely flammable,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.640
safety's always a concern.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.560
Just look at the hydrogen Zeppelins

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.033
of the early 20th century.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.270
But if we can overcome the economic

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.070
and engineering challenges,

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.680
the most abundant element in the universe

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:38.100
could provide the abundant energy we need.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.710
- I want to see a decarbonized future

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:44.820
but I'm also an engineer, a
practicalist, and a realist.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.360
And that transition to a low carbon future

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.460
is not going to happen overnight.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.880
We need realistic transition options

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.370
and technologies that
allow us to decarbonize

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.963
our existing applications.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:00.760
- I love this company.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.420
I love the people I work with

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.090
and we're all in this together

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:10.263
for this almost sacred mission.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.250
This is our duty for our children

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:15.510
and for our grandchildren,

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:17.190
and for generations to come.

